BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR AN AUTHOR SOCIAL WITH PATRICK FRIESEN\, EVE JOSEPH AND
  TERENCE YOUNG
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260927T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260927T203000
UID:bm-6217620260927@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/eve.png?c
 b=1787861591
DESCRIPTION:[Author Social]\nJoin us for an Author Social with Patrick Fri
 esen\, Eve Joseph and Terence Young.   Admission is free\, but space is 
 limited. Please RSVP to attend.\nWe’re delighted to welcome these three
  writers to Flying Books at Neverland to celebrate their latest books:\nSi
 ghtings\,\nDismantling\nand\nGive Us This Day\n.\n\nFriesen’s\nSightings
 \nmoves between memory\, music\, history and the present\, while Joseph’
 s\nDismantling\nexplores language and imagination\, and the ways a poem ca
 n be taken apart and rebuilt. Young’s\nGive Us This Day\nis a collection
  of short fiction that explores how the past shapes identity—and how it 
 can continue to follow us.\nPatrick Friesen is the author of nearly twenty
  collections of poetry\, including the influential\nThe\nShunning\n. Winne
 r of a ReLit Award and Manitoba Book of the Year award\, he has also been\
 nshortlisted for the Governor General’s award and Griffin Poety Prize. B
 orn in Manitoba\, he now lives\non the unceded traditional territories of 
 the Lekwungen peoples\, known today as the Esquimalt and\nSonghees First N
 ations in Victoria\, British Columbia.\nEve Joseph lives and works on the 
 unceded traditional territories of the Lekwungen peoples. Her\nfirst two b
 ooks of poetry\nThe Startled Heart\nand\nThe Secret Signature of Things\nw
 ere both\nnominated for the Dorothy Livesay Award. She was awarded the P.K
 . Page Founder’s Award for\npoetry in 2010. Her nonfiction has been shor
 tlisted for the CBC Literary Awards and won in the\n“Gold” category of
  the Western Magazine Awards. The first edition of I\nn the Slender Margin
 \,\npublished by HarperCollins in 2014\, won the Hubert Evans Nonfiction P
 rize and was named one of\nthe Top 100 Books of the Year by the Globe and 
 Mail. Her most recent book of poetry\nQuarrels\n(Anvil\, 2018) was nominat
 ed for the Dorothy Livesay Prize\, The ReLit Award\, and won the 2019\nGri
 ffin Poetry Prize.\nTerence Young is a writer and poet from Victoria BC. H
 is first book of poetry\,\nThe Island\nin Winter\,\nwas shortlisted for th
 e Governor General’s Award and the Gerald Lampert Award. Since then\, he
  has\npublished several books:\nRhymes With Useless\,\nwhich was a finalis
 t for the Danuta Gleed award\;\nAfter Goodlake’s\, which received the Ci
 ty of Victoria Butler Book Prize\; and\nMoving Day\,\nwhich was\nnominated
  for both the Dorothy Livesay Poetry Prize and The City of Victoria Butler
  Book Prize.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MAKE IT MAKE SENSE: THE FLYING BOOKS NONFICTION BOOKCLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T203000
UID:bm-6051920260929@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Copy_of_S
 eptember_Non_Fiction_Book_Club_.png?cb=1786112140
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nJoin Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCity in Flames\n\
 , on Tuesday\, September 29th\, from 6:30 to 8:30 pm for a lively discussi
 on of\nTrash\nby Simon Paré-Poupart\, translated by \nPablo Strauss.\nA M
 ontreal garbageman's sharp and funny memoir\, in which he attempts to conv
 ince people to "stop imagining that your garbage magically disappears" . .
  . A fascinating no-bullshit account of twenty years in waste management p
 aints a vivid portrait of the heroic labor\, anarchic spirit\, and violent
  conditions of the people who keep our cities clean.\nParé-Poupart’s st
 ory is atypical: he started working as a garbageman to pay for school\, an
 d after earning graduate degrees and working in more “respectable” fie
 lds\, he is still on a truck — out of love for the physical rush\, for h
 is rough-and-tumble colleagues\, and for an honesty and freedom that no ot
 her job has yet given him.\nJoin us on Tuesday\, Septemeber 29th. We hope 
 to see you there!\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\nYour book club ticket includes:
 \nA glass of wine (or another beverage of your choice).\nA thoughtful conv
 ersation with Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCity in Flames\n.\nA $20 credit t
 oward any book in the shop.\nA few details:\nTicket price includes HST and
  fees.\n$20 book credit is valid on the night of attendance only (non-refu
 ndable and non-transferable).\nWine is served on the night of the event (I
 D required).\nTickets are non-refundable within 7 days of the event\; due 
 to limited capacity and planning\, we’re not able to transfer tickets to
  future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF ON PERSISTENCE
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T200000
UID:bm-6198920260929@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/On_Persis
 tence_poster_FINAL.jpg?cb=1787883844
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Rebecca Comay for the lau
 nch of her new book\nOn Persistence.\nRebecca will be in conversation with
  Ian Balfour\, and the event will be moderated by Cary Fagan. Books will b
 e on sale.\nA bold meditation on endurance as interruption rather than pro
 gress\, offering a radical rethinking of time\, agency\, and survival.\nOn
  Persistence\nis an exploration into the elusive\, unruly force of enduran
 ce—not as moral virtue or capitalist work ethic\, but as a temporal and 
 affective structure that resists assimilation. In these wide-ranging philo
 sophical and literary essays\, Rebecca Comay interrogates what it means to
  persist when progress stalls and resolution fails to arrive. Engaging fig
 ures from Freud to Beckett\, Proust to Benjamin\, Hamlet to Niobe\, Comay 
 tracks the strange temporality of suspended life: interminable mourning\, 
 unfinished projects\, the persistence of the past\, the undead stirrings o
 f the death drive. What emerges is a radically different model of time—n
 either static nor teleological\, but a form of interruption that opens spa
 ce for rethinking agency\, grief\, resistance\, and repair.\nOn Persistenc
 e\noffers a profound meditation on what it means to endure—and to endure
  differently—in a time that demands something else of us.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF HAIL MARY BY ELIZABETH URSO
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260930T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260930T200000
UID:bm-6181420260930@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/EUrso_HAI
 L_MARY_Flying_Books_Launch_Graphic.png?cb=1787573917
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and River Street Writes to ce
 lebrate the Toronto launch of\nHail Mary\nby debut Canadian author Elizabe
 th Urso. Elizabeth will be joined by bestselling\nCanadian author Ashley T
 ate. Admission is free\, but space is limited. Please RSVP to\nattend.\nPi
 tched as\nSix Feet Under\nby way of\nRebecca\,\nHail Mary\nfollows Mary Fo
 reman\, who\nreturns to her hometown to help her estranged family during a
  scandal-plagued election.\nBut when the opening of a decades-old time cap
 sule draws her into a criminal\ninvestigation tied to her family’s funer
 al empire\, she must fight to prove her innocence.\nNot only of recent cri
 mes\, put past ones\, too. For this is the North Shore of Lake Huron\,\nan
 d the past is always present where the dead don’t die.\nElizabeth Urso i
 s a graduate of Laurentian University\, the University of New Brunswick\nF
 aculty of Law\, and the Humber School for Writers. Born and raised in Sudb
 ury\,\nOntario\, she now lives wherever her partner—a member of the Cana
 dian Armed\nForces—is posted\, along with their dog Harriet (the Spy).\n
 Ashley Tate worked for over a decade as a writer and an editor for various
  publications\nas well as Canada’s first online magazine. Twenty-Seven M
 inutes\, the instant #1\nNational Bestseller and winner of the Crime Write
 rs of Canada award for best first crime\nnovel\, is her debut. She lives w
 ith her husband\, their two children\, and their dog in\nToronto.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF THE DUES AND DON'TS OF MAGIC
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T200000
UID:bm-6312520261006@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Book_Laun
 ch___Dues_and_Donts_IG_POST.jpg?cb=1789603284
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Simon & Schuster Canada f
 or the launch of T\nhe Dues and Don'ts of Magic\nby Delemhach\, moderated 
 by Victoria S. Walsh\, author o\nf The Iron Hex.\nThe Spellsho\np\nmeets\n
 Legends & Lattes i\nn this new cozy romantic fantasy trilogy about magic g
 one wrong\, by the bestselling author of\nThe House Witch\nseries!\nSpiden
 a Kettle runs a quiet magic shop at the edge of town\, selling just enough
  spells and potions to keep herself afloat—and out of trouble—when Ben
 throp Hozel enters her shop one day. On the run from the nefarious sort\, 
 he simply wishes to be forgotten by his pursuers. In exchange\, he promise
 s a favor.\n\nSuch a deal could have dire consequences if Ben is unable to
  fulfill his end of the bargain. Not that the witch is overly concerned—
 after all\, there’s no risk to her making such an arrangement with a nor
 mal human. Only…Ben may be more than he seems. With their fates forcibly
  intertwined for a full journey\, Spidena and Ben must not only evade thei
 r enemies and use precious magical resources to get by\, but confront the 
 secrets they’ve long kept…that is\, if they can manage to stop getting
  on each other’s last nerve for long enough to do so.\nDelemhach—the p
 seudonym for Emilie Nikota—is the bestselling romantic fantasy author of
 \nThe House Wit\nch series\,\nThe Burning Witch\nseries\, and\nThe Ether W
 itch\nseries. Born and raised in Canada\, she discovered fantasy and magic
  at a young age and began posting her stories online to share the warmth\,
  fun\, and love of food she had growing up. She has since gone on to self-
 publish to great success\, with hundreds of thousands of copies sold throu
 ghout the world.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR AN AUTHOR SOCIAL WITH MARK ANTHONY JARMAN
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T203000
UID:bm-6345120261006@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/WW_2.png?
 cb=1790007138
DESCRIPTION:[Author Social]\nCome hear Mark Anthony Jarman in conversation
  with Flying Books publisher Martha Sharpe as they discuss the making of\n
 Wild West\n.\nTwenty-six years ago\, Mark’s story “Skin a Flea for Hid
 e and Tallow\,” about a nameless recruit at the Battle of the Little Big
 horn\, appeared in his collection\n19 Knives\,\nedited by Martha. Intrigue
 d by the history and voice\, Martha kept asking Mark about the character\,
  and over the years Mark sent her more stories about this same everyman. M
 artha was sure this guy could be the basis of a novel. Mark resisted\; Mar
 tha persisted. The result is\nWild West\n: brutal and tender\, honest and 
 funny\, a wildly original novel close to three decades in the making that 
 throws Canada in a startling new light.\nMark and Martha will talk about t
 he creative process behind\nWild West\n– from the original idea to the f
 inished novel. They’ll discuss what it’s like to work on a book for mo
 re than 25 years and how the work evolved along the way.\nMARK ANTHONY JAR
 MAN is the author of twelve books\, including\nBurn Man\n\,\n19 Knives\,\n
 and\nSmash and Grab\n. A graduate of the Iowa Writers’ Workshop\, Jarman
  has taught at the University of Victoria\, the Banff Centre\, and Univers
 ity of New Brunswick. He is co-editor of the literary journal CAMEL.\n\nMA
 RTHA SHARPE is the owner and publisher of Flying Books.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF CANTALOUPE
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:bm-6181520261014@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/cantaloup
 e.jpg?cb=1787688013
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nCantaloupe!\nbrings us nine juicy stories about
  trying (and failing) to do the right thing — about\ncharacters who stri
 ve to hold everything together while staying true to their convictions and
  sense of\njustice in the process. With razor-sharp prose and a bit of sla
 pstick humour\, M. Jay Smith contemplates\nhow identities smudge into each
  other\, how they shout and push and wither and explode under\npressure. A
 nd no matter what\, one’s best intentions go a little bit awry.\n“M. J
 ay Smith writes with rare honesty about ordinary people.— Peter Midgley
 \, author of Let Us Not\nThink of Them As Barbarians\n“M. Jay Smith’s
  wry stories surprise at every turn to wondrously reveal the anguish and e
 xhilaration\nrunning through our faltering\, fluttering human hearts.” 
 — Mikka Jacobsen\, author of Good Victory
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR OUR OCTOBER FICTION BOOK CLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T203000
UID:bm-6345920261019@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Oct_Fic_B
 C_2026.jpg?cb=1789164902
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nFrom the Booker Prize–winning author of\nA Brie
 f History of Seven Killings\n\, Marlon James is back with another longlist
 ed title.\nIn\nThe Disappearers\n\, James has written a riveting and deepl
 y human story of men forced to make compromises to survive that the societ
 y they live in demands. It is both a dramatic page-turner and an unflinchi
 ng exploration of queer life in Jamaica during the 1980s and 1990s.\nJoin 
 us on Monday\, October 19th and Tuesday\, October 20th for a thrilling con
 versation. We hope to see you there.\nYour book club ticket includes:\nA g
 lass of wine (or another beverage of your choice).\nA thoughtful conversat
 ion with our brilliant moderator\, Lee Suksi.\nA $20 credit toward any boo
 k in the shop.*\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\nA few details:\nTicket price incl
 udes HST and fees.\n*$20 book credit is valid on the night of attendance o
 nly (non-refundable and non-transferable).\nWine is served on the night of
  the event (ID required).\nTickets are non-refundable within 7 days of the
  event\; due to limited capacity and planning\, we’re not able to transf
 er tickets to future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MAKE IT MAKE SENSE: THE FLYING BOOKS NONFICTION BOOKCLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261025T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261025T203000
UID:bm-6443520261025@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Oct_Nonfi
 c_Book_Club_2026.jpg?cb=1790375582
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nJoin Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCity in Flames\n\
 , on Sunday\, October 25th\, from 6:30 to 8:30 pm for a lively discussion 
 of\nThe Reverse Centaur’s Guide to Life After AI\nby Cory Doctorow.\nA s
 hort\, provocative guide to what's good\, bad\, and stupid about AI and th
 e discourse around AI\, by the author of\nEnshittification\n.\nThe Reverse
  Centaur’s Guide to Life After AI\nis not another anti-AI screed. Cory D
 octorow uses AI in his work every day. As a creative person\, he has no mo
 ral or dogmatic issue with it—he thinks the technology is useful\, even 
 exciting\, and full of potential. And yet.\nFor Doctorow\, it is imperativ
 e to see through the hype to the real story\, to understand the technology
  not just for what it does\, but for who it does it to and who for. Doctor
 ow recounts both how we found ourselves in this dire situation and how we 
 can get through it\, to a life “after” AI in which the tools work for 
 us\, not the other way around.\nJoin us on Sunday\, October 25th for an ev
 ening of conversation. We hope to see you there.\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\n
 Your book club ticket includes:\nA glass of wine (or another beverage of y
 our choice).\nA thoughtful conversation with Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCi
 ty in Flames\n.\nA $20 credit toward any book in the shop.\nA few details:
 \nTicket price includes HST and fees.\n$20 book credit is valid on the nig
 ht of attendance only (non-refundable and non-transferable).\nWine is serv
 ed on the night of the event (ID required).\nTickets are non-refundable wi
 thin 7 days of the event\; due to limited capacity and planning\, we’re 
 not able to transfer tickets to future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF WHAT'S A SNOW LEOPARD TO YOU?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261102T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261102T200000
UID:bm-6419220261102@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/ChenFuHsu
 .png?cb=1790169605
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Skyhorse for a conversati
 on between Chen-Fu Hsu\, author of\nWhat’s a Snow Leopard to You?\nand L
 ee Suksi\, author of\nThe Nerves.\n*Winner of the 2021 Taiwan Literature A
 ward*\nWhat’s a Snow Leopard to You? i\ns a captivating blend of travel 
 writing\, fiction\, history\, and nature.\n\nFollow a young Taiwanese natu
 ralist into the breathtaking landscapes of Tibet in search of the elusive 
 snow leopard\, while a long-lost monk’s diary offers a window onto a cha
 nging Tibet and its rich cultural history.\n\nBeautifully written and rich
 ly layered\, Chen-Fu Hsu’s debut explores landscape\, memory\, wildlife\
 , and our complicated relationship with the natural world.\nChen-Fu Hsu (
 徐振輔) is a Taiwanese writer whose work explores the entanglements bet
 ween human and non-human worlds. He holds a BS in Entomology and an MA in 
 Geography from National Taiwan University. His debut novel\,\nWhat’s a S
 now Leopard to You?\n\, has received multiple literary prizes in Taiwan.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF WHOOSH
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T200000
UID:bm-6433220261112@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Whoosh.pn
 g?cb=1790278585
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Goose Lane for the launch
  of\nWhoosh\nby Michael Trussler\, featuring special guests Cassidy McFadz
 ean and Daniel Scott Tysdal\, hosted by Jim Johnstone.\nPart exploration o
 f ecological collapse\, part memoir undressing a late-life Autism diagnosi
 s.\n\nMichael Trussler’s\nWhoosh\nis an unwavering collection of “ramb
 les / more rogue than feral.” Bypassing the limitations of linear though
 t\, this unforgettable book is a maelstrom of the contemporary moment that
 ’s simultaneously urgent and adrift\, floating and incisive.\n\nTrussler
  draws influence from modern artistic movements\, political insecurity\, t
 he internet\, and environmental grief to dovetail both the unexpected and 
 the intimately familiar. Lyric and experimental\, these blazing poems coll
 apse in on themselves and each other\, lines and thoughts expanding and co
 lliding to perfectly embody the connectedness of our world’s chaos.\nMic
 hael Trussler’s work engages with the beauty and violence of the twentie
 th and twenty-first centuries from a neurodivergent\, fluid perspective. H
 is writing encompasses several genres and modes of expression\, ranging fr
 om the lyrical to the avant-garde. Trussler teaches English at the Univers
 ity of Regina and is the author of seven books\, including 1\n0:10\; The H
 istory Forest\n\, winner of the Saskatchewan Book Award for Poetry\; the s
 hort fiction collection\nEncounters\,\nwinner of the Saskatchewan Book of 
 the Year Award\; and a memoir entitled\nThe Sunday Book\,\nwhich won the S
 askatchewan Book Award in both the Non-Fiction and City of Regina categori
 es. Deeply compelled by the natural world\, Trussler hikes in the Canadian
  Rockies at every opportunity.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
